you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  disruption in westminster as climate  protesters block streets in the second  day of demonstrations  confirmation from scientists that  climate change did make last month's  deadly floods in europe more likely  and the nature inspired green materials  being used in climate-friendly batteries  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show now it's day two of  climate protests by extinction rebellion  with activists blocking roads around  parliament square and targeting the hmrc  building in westminster  protesters want the government to  immediately end all investment in fossil  fuels but are the demonstrations likely  to make a difference to government  policy or turn people away from the  cause altogether our climate  correspondent hannah thomas peter  reports  extinction rebellion protesters in  central london  racing to block whitehall  or part of a two-week plan to disrupt  the capital  today the target is the treasury and  government funding of the fossil fuel  industry it's not just about the  dividend at the end of the year it's  about making sure that there is an end  of the year and the next year and our  children have got a future to look  forward to it's one thing buying organic  food from the health food shop on the  corner what difference does it make if  your money is going into the fossil fuel  industry  the police have warned that this kind of  disruption is pulling them away from  communities in need the mayor of london  has said that he supports the right to  democratic and peaceful protest and that  climate change is something that needs  tackling with increasing urgency but  he's also warned that disruptive actions  that break the law will only be  counterproductive to the course  these protesters don't agree are you  potentially overshadowing your message  by causing so much disruption i think  that the disruption currently caused by  forest fires and floods are already  causing more disruption than this this  is tiny this is negligible really i know  that we're causing a lot of disruption i  really know that  but the disruption that if we don't do  something it's just going to be such  it's going to be catastrophic the whole  planet's in crisis and i just feel like  the time is really running out  although in terms of sheer numbers  extinction rebellion isn't the force it  once was it is still a determined one  however unlikely it is to shift  government policy or the behavior of big  business it has many more plans to stop  business as usual in the nation's  capital hannah thomas peter sky news  climate change made the catastrophic  floods in europe last month up to nine  times more likely that's according to  rapid analysis by an international group  of scientists parts of germany belgium  the netherlands and luxembourg were  devastated after rain caused severe  flooding back in july more than 200  people lost their lives and homes  businesses and infrastructure were all  destroyed well the world weather  attribution group carried out the  analysis of the floods and found the  extreme rainfall events that caused them  are now between 1.2 and 9 times more  likely to happen because of global  warming and you can see here how the  extreme rain swept across western europe  it started in england on july the 11th  before moving across france and belgium  and reaching germany by the 13th of july  now by analyzing weather records and  using computer simulations scientists  found that downpours like this are now  between 3 and 19  heavier in the region than when the  world was 1.2 degrees cooler and that  1.2 degree number is important because  it's how much our planet has warmed  since the industrial revolution and you  can see here on our data dashboard how  global warming is ticking up the whole  time making extreme weather events more  likely as well as more severe  well one of the lead authors of that  report is dr sarah q a climate scientist  from the royal netherlands  meteorological institute so welcome to  you  your study then showed that climate  change did make the extreme rainfall  that we saw more likely but there was a  big range in that likelihood wasn't  there between 1.2 times and nine times  more likely why the breadth of that  range  yeah that's right and so our results  highlight that at the local river  catchment scale that the detection of  trends and extreme rainfall is hindered  by the large day-to-day and year-to-year  variability in the rainfall data  but when we considered similar events  occurring over a larger western european  uh western european region so between  the north and northern side of the alps  and the netherlands we found that uh  significant trends due to human-induced  climate change then became evident  so what  uh yeah the results um therefore show  that there's a relation with climate  change and that's the most important  conclusion but we need more research  more model data and more high resolution  models to be able to reduce the  uncertainty range  and factor one point there actually  means there's already been a 20  increase in frequency of these kinds of  events which is really important for  impacts  well yes and what can be done to reduce  the likelihood of this sort of event in  the future then  well there have always been extreme  weather events that occur now and again  so that will also be the case in the  future however we see that climate  change is making extreme events like  these more frequent and more intense and  the best thing to do about this is to  rapidly reduce those greenhouse gas  emissions  but we need to be aware that greenhouse  gases will stay in the atmosphere for  hundreds of years so in our lifetimes it  will be a matter of not exacerbating the  climate change any further rather than  being able to strongly reduce it  but adaptation will also help to reduce  the impact of extreme weather on  people's lives and also on the  infrastructure their livelihoods so it's  important for water boards and  authorities for example to know what we  can expect in the future  and dikes can be reinforced or flood  buffer zones created in the locations  where that is possible  also warning systems are obviously very  high importance and reaching out to our  vulnerable neighbours  dr sarah q thanks very much indeed  thank you  well in today's other climate news more  than 13 000 firefighters are attempting  to contain a dozen large wildfires that  have destroyed hundreds of buildings and  forced people to evacuate their homes in  california hot and dry conditions made  worse by climate change have caused the  fires to spread rapidly scientists are  warning that wildfires will become more  frequent as global warming intensifies  development work at the site of the  proposed cambo oil field has been  delayed until next year it comes after  greenpeace claimed that work was  starting early before permits had been  issued sicar point energy said  operational issues were behind the delay  the government's been criticized for  letting plans for the site in the north  sea go ahead despite hosting the cop 26  climate summit in november  a demonstration flight of what could be  the uk's first electric commuter plane  successfully took off from exeter today  the flight which ended at cornwall's new  key airport is the start of low carbon  test flights between the two counties if  the trials are successful hybrid  electric planes which produce 70 fewer  emissions than traditional aircraft  could become a more common sight in uk  skies  now here on the daily climate show we do  love to feature people coming up with  solutions to the climate crisis and one  of those is professor magda titus  who's just won a royal society award for  her work on sustainable energy storage  well i'll be speaking to her in just a  moment but first let's take a look at  what she's been working on  [Music]  and professor magda titurichi from  imperial college london joins me now  first of all many congratulations on  your award and first of all let me ask  you about the area of your work that  focuses on lignin as an alternative to  lithium for use in batteries like  electric car batteries  tell us what lignum is and why we need  an alternative to lithium  yeah so lignin is one of the components  in any plants  along with cellulose and hemi cellulose  for example when we're using plants to  make paper  uh we are after the cellulosic fraction  as well as when we want to make biofuels  from from biomass and lignin is the one  the fraction that nobody wants they is  normally of no use and we found the  solution and we take this lignin which  is actually responsible of giving uh  good strength and mechanical properties  to plant and taking inspiration from  nature a little bit like the spider  spice the web we spy we spin this lignin  into fibers which you can make  conductive and a good mechanical  properties and we can use this as  electrodes in pretty much any battery  whether it's a lithium or sodium battery  and so it can be either the anode which  is the negative electrode but it could  also be on both sides for for particular  uh battery applications and instead of  lithium we are then using some metallic  sodium clusters and we can produce  batteries with an energy density that is  higher than what lithium ions have today  so how much greener is the production  then of lignin and how easy and cost  effective might it be to get it to  market  so the lignin production is quite easy  because we are doing this since we've  discovered paper this is produced in in  huge amounts of megatons a year in all  the paper and plants industry and and  now in all the biorefineries and so it's  a widely available uh resources  and it's almost available at no cost  again to produce the materials from  lignin you use relatively low energy  input low temperatures in particular we  figure out that the lower the  temperature that we use the better the  performance of this material is  so it is uh obviously most  cost-effective than uh lithium-ion  battery today which relies on critical  materials that are mined somewhere else  rather than in the uk  fascinating stuff uh professor magda  titarichi thanks very much indeed  okay  and finally sydney has announced dark  roofs will be banned on new homes in an  effort to combat climate change in  australia lighter colored surfaces  retain less heat resulting in less  electricity being used to cool down  buildings the new rules also mean that  every garden will be required to have a  tree to provide shade and absorb carbon  dioxide  and that's everything from us for today  tomorrow find out why climate change  could mean uk summer holidays are under  threat thanks for watching and see you  next time  [Music]

